### 2.4 Reframing Hypothesis (Phase 1 Data Test Required)

> The regulatory architecture of Indian DPI welfare cases inscribes citizens into records but not into rules — they appear in the system as data subjects but not as rights-bearing parties in the enforceable obligations.

This is the records–rules asymmetry framing. It has four components, all requiring data verification before the extended abstract is drafted:

1. Citizens are inscribed as records (data subjects) — **likely confirmed** by existing corpus (Aadhaar, AA, PM-KISAN, PM-JAY all do this)
2. Citizens are not inscribed as rule-bearing parties in enforceable obligations — **confirmed by existing finding** (0 of 11 Rules govern citizen-facing dimensions)
3. Pattern holds across all four cases — **requires explicit tabulation** (rerun existing coded output: count statements where citizen is Attribute in ADICO terms vs. statements where citizen appears as data attribute)
4. The records–rules framing is more illuminating than the current technical-citizen framing — **judgement call after steps 1–3**

If 4/4 components hold → proceed with records–rules framing.
If 3/4 hold → proceed with conservative framing; exception becomes a boundary condition.
If ≤2/4 hold → retain original technical-citizen rule allocation asymmetry framing. Plan still works.

